    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa.jpg" alt="Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_las_Navas_de_Tolosa,_por_Francisco_van_Halen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco de Paula Van Halen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Banu Marin were a nomadic faction of the Zenata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> confederation who originated in the Zab, the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. Pushed from their lands by the arrival of Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouins</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries, they moved to north-western Algeria and by the beginning of the 13th century entered what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in force. The dynasty took its name from Marin ibn Wartajan al-Zenati, and like earlier Berber dynasties its historians claimed a North Arabian origin to help legitimise its rule. Although Berber, the Marinid rulers were culturally Arabised.</p><p>They began as servants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>. Their leader Mahyu fought for the Almohad victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> in 1195 and died of his wounds; his son Abd al-Haqq, born in the Zab to a noble family, was the effective founder of the dynasty. The Almohad disaster at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> on 16 July 1212 left their state weakened and parts of it depopulated, and from 1213 or 1214 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> began taxing the farming communities of north-eastern Morocco between Nador and Berkane. Fighting with the Almohads broke out regularly from 1215; in 1217 an attempt on eastern Morocco failed and Abd al-Haqq was killed. Under Uthman I and Muhammad I the tribe regrouped and built its authority over the rural tribes around Taza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and Ksar el-Kebir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Sa'id beat the Marinids back south of Taza in 1244, the year Abu Yahya's reign began.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/granada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/granada.jpg" alt="Granada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Granada_(25987961022).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brett Hodnett, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The population under Marinid rule was mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Arab, divided between heavily Arabised and uniformly Islamised cities, where politics ran along the lines of local aristocratic families, and a countryside that stayed largely Berber and tribal; the nomads, Berber tribes joined by Arab ones such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> who had arrived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, became more Arabised than the settled peasantry. Jewish communities were a significant urban minority active in most aspects of society: the Jewish quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> el-Jdid, the first mellah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, came into being in this period. Berber was the language of the court and of rural life, Arabic the language of law, government and most literature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabisation">Arabisation</a> advanced significantly.</p><p>Fez had been prosperous under the Almohads, even the largest city in the world, but it was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> that it reached its golden age and acquired its reputation as an intellectual centre. The sultans sought control of the commercial traffic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, which the Nasrids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> had ceded to them, with shipyards at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>. Luxurious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> continued to be made, though the only reliably dated Marinid textiles are three banners captured at Río Salado in 1340 and kept in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> chandeliers hung in the mosques, the one at Taza of 1294 weighing three tons and holding 514 lamps.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado.jpg" alt="Battle of Río Salado" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_batalla_del_Salado_(1340).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Marinid army was built of tribes. Its core was the Banu Marin and their allies, supplemented by other Zenata tribes of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, by Arab tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and Banu Ma'qil, and by Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> hired as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> had hired them, mainly cavalry who served as the sultan's bodyguard. Under Abu al-Hasan the main striking force was some 40,000 Zenata horsemen with Arab tribal cavalry, about 1,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> of 'Turkish' origin and about 1,000 Andalusi foot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, while the standing army and royal guard numbered 2,000 to 5,000 salaried mercenaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal with Black Africans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>. The fleet was the weak point: it could not keep up with Aragon's.</p><p>With this army the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1269, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> for nine years from 1299 and fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> Abu Yusuf Yaqub campaigned against Castile, dying there in 1286 after his fourth expedition, and by 1294 the Marinids held Rota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. Then came the reverses: a Portuguese–Castilian coalition crushed Abu al-Hasan's army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a> near Tarifa in 1340, and in 1344 Castile took Algeciras, ending the Marinid presence in Iberia. Abu al-Hasan took Tlemcen in 1337 and beat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> in 1347, holding the Maghreb to Tripoli for a year until a revolt of Arab tribes in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> stripped away the east.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/madrasa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrasa.jpg" alt="Madrasa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain; derivative work by MrPanyGoff, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In many respects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> continued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> order: a primarily tribal state resting on the loyalty of its own tribe and allies, with very little civil administration in the provinces beyond the capital. They kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tradition of consultative government through a council of Marinid tribal chiefs whom the sultan consulted, chiefly on military matters, and held the provinces by appointing family members as governors or by marriage alliances. The sultan bore the title amir al-muslimin, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-al-muslimin">Commander of the Muslims</a>', and later sultans sometimes took amir al-mu'minin, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>'; the heir apparent often led the army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> ran the day-to-day government.</p><p>Religion was a tool of legitimacy. The Marinids presented themselves as guardians of proper Islamic government, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> Sunnism as the official religion after Almohadism, allied with the Maliki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> of the cities and with the sharifian families, with whom they sometimes intermarried, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> insisted on appointing the officials of religious institutions and managing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">waqf</a> endowments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> they built, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, trained the Maliki scholars who staffed their bureaucracy and were meant to undermine the marabouts and sharifian elements. Families of viziers competed for power, the Wattasids above all, and after 1358 they made and unmade sultans.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun.jpg" alt="Ibn Khaldun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Ibn_Khaldun_(Casbah_of_Bejaia,_Algeria).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reda Kerbush, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> remade the religious and intellectual landscape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. They pioneered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> there, a type of institution born in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by the early 11th century that had been antithetical to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> doctrine: the Saffarin Madrasa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1271 was the first, the al-Attarine followed in 1323, and the Bou Inania, the largest, was also a Friday mosque, with others from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> to Taza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">Qarawiyyin</a> reached its apogee in prestige and scope, and the age produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">fiqh</a>, poetry, science, geography and above all history, in part because the dynasty wanted histories to legitimise it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> was the most famous figure of this intellectual life, shared with Nasrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> set out on his 29-year journey in 1325.</p><p>In architecture the Marinids refined the Almoravid and Almohad legacy with ever more intricate wood and stucco and were the first to use zellij mosaic tilework extensively, which became standard in Moroccan building; their style was closely akin to the Nasrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>. Their madrasas in Fez, Salé and Meknes are counted among the greatest works of western Islamic architecture of the period, beside the Grand Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fes-el-jdid">Fes el-Jdid</a> (1276), the Great Mosque of Taza (1294) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a> necropolis gate of 1339. The growing weight of sharifian families and the popular veneration of the Idrisids under their rule prepared the way for the Saadians and Alawis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris.jpg" alt="Abu Inan Faris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris">Abu Inan Faris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Inan_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline began at the height. In 1348 Abu al-Hasan, who had lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> to the Arab revolt, was deposed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris">Abu Inan Faris</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> and the rebellions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> marked the turn; in 1358 Abu Inan was strangled by his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. Real power now lay with the viziers, who paraded sultans and forced them to succeed one another in quick succession: Abu Salim Ibrahim in 1359 with the backing of Pedro of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Muhammad ibn Yaqub, assassinated by his vizier in 1366. The country split: in 1359 Hintata tribesmen from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, which they ruled until 1526; in 1374 the empire was partitioned into kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and Marrakesh; and wandering Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> spread anarchy.</p><p>The 15th century brought a financial crisis that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to the marabouts and sharifian families, and the Christian powers struck the coast: Henry III of Castile seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> in 1399, massacred half its people and enslaved the rest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">John I of Portugal</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1415. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-haqq-ii">Abd al-Haqq II</a> became sultan in 1420 at the age of one, and the Wattasid viziers ruled as regents. In 1459 he massacred the Wattasid family; in 1465 his appointment of a Jewish vizier, Aaron ben Batash, provoked a popular revolt in Fez in which his throat was cut. In 1471–72 the surviving Wattasid Abu Abd Allah al-Sheikh Muhammad ibn Yahya installed himself in Fez and founded the Wattasid dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Marinid Sultanate, a Zenata Berber dynasty, replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> between 1244 and 1269 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>'s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and reaching under Abu al-Hasan from Morocco to Tripoli, until defeat at Río Salado, the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and the Wattasid regency ended with the last sultan's murder in Fez in 1465.</em></p>

